---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import BodyFatCalculator from '@components/islands/BodyFatCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'body-fat-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How does the U.S. Navy method work?',
    answer:
      'It estimates body fat from circumferences rather than weight. For men it uses neck, waist and height; for women it adds the hip. The idea is that where a tape sits tells you more about fat than a scale can, because it distinguishes a thick waist from a heavy frame.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why do the Navy and BMI figures disagree?',
    answer:
      'Because they measure different things. The Navy method reads where fat actually sits. The BMI method knows only height, weight and age, so it cannot tell muscle from fat — a muscular person reads high and someone light but soft reads low. A gap of a percentage point or two is normal; a large one usually means an unusual amount of muscle.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How accurate is this?',
    answer:
      'It is an estimate, typically within about 3–4 percentage points of a DEXA scan for most people, and less reliable at the extremes. Its real strength is consistency: measured the same way each time, it tracks a direction over months far better than it pins down a single number today.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How should I measure?',
    answer:
      'To the nearest quarter inch or half centimetre, with a flexible tape, standing relaxed and not holding your breath. Neck just below the larynx, sloping slightly downward at the front. Waist at the navel for men, at the narrowest point for women. Hip at the widest point. Measure at the same time of day each time — a full stomach can move the waist by a centimetre.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a healthy body fat percentage?',
    answer:
      'For men, roughly 2–5% is essential fat, 6–13% athletic, 14–17% fitness, 18–24% average and 25%+ obese. For women the figures are higher because of essential reproductive fat: about 10–13% essential, 14–20% athletic, 21–24% fitness, 25–31% average and 32%+ obese. The gauge places your reading on that scale.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the ideal figure this shows?',
    answer:
      'It is Jackson & Pollock\'s reference value for your age and sex — the percentage typical of a healthy person of those years, not a target you must hit. It rises with age for both sexes, which is normal and expected.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which unit tab should I use?',
    answer:
      'Whichever matches your tape and scales. US Units takes pounds and feet with inches; Metric Units takes kilograms and centimetres; Other Units takes stones and pounds, metres, and inches for the girths. Switching tabs converts what you have already typed, so nothing is lost.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Estimate your body fat from a tape measure using the U.S. Navy method, with your category, fat and lean mass, and a second estimate from BMI."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-17' }}
>
  <BodyFatCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick the <strong>unit tab</strong> that matches your tape and scales. Switching tabs
        converts what you have already typed rather than clearing it.</li>
      <li>Choose your <strong>gender</strong> and enter your <strong>age</strong> — both change
        which formula and which reference figures apply.</li>
      <li>Enter <strong>weight</strong>, <strong>height</strong>, <strong>neck</strong> and
        <strong>waist</strong>. Women are also asked for the <strong>hip</strong>, because the
        female formula uses it.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> for your percentage, your category on the scale, and
        the full report.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How to measure</h2>
    <p>
      The result is only as good as the tape. Measure to the nearest quarter inch or half
      centimetre, standing relaxed, without holding your breath or pulling the tape tight.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Neck</strong> — just below the larynx, sloping slightly downward at the front.</li>
      <li><strong>Waist</strong> — at the navel for men; at the narrowest point for women.</li>
      <li><strong>Hip</strong> — at the widest point, feet together.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Measure at the same time of day each time. A full stomach can move a waist by a centimetre,
      which is enough to shift the percentage by half a point.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 25-year-old man weighing <strong>70 kg</strong> at <strong>178 cm</strong>, with a
      <strong>50 cm</strong> neck and a <strong>96 cm</strong> waist, comes out at
      <strong>15.7%</strong> body fat — the <strong>Fitness</strong> range. That is
      <strong>11.0 kg</strong> of fat against <strong>59.0 kg</strong> of lean mass. The typical
      figure for his age is 10.5%, so he is carrying about <strong>3.6 kg</strong> more fat than
      that reference. The BMI method puts him at 16.1%, half a point higher.
    </p>

    <h2>Why two estimates, and why they differ</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>Navy method</strong> reads circumferences, so it knows something about where
      fat sits. The <strong>BMI method</strong> knows only height, weight and age, and cannot
      tell muscle from fat — which is why a heavily muscled person reads high on it and someone
      light but untrained reads low. Showing both, and the gap between them, says more than
      either would alone. A point or two apart is ordinary; a wide gap usually means an unusual
      amount of muscle.
    </p>

    <h2>What the categories mean</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Category</th><th>Men</th><th>Women</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Essential fat</td><td>2–5%</td><td>10–13%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Athletes</td><td>6–13%</td><td>14–20%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fitness</td><td>14–17%</td><td>21–24%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Average</td><td>18–24%</td><td>25–31%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Obese</td><td>25%+</td><td>32%+</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Women's figures are higher throughout, and that is biology rather than fitness: essential
      reproductive fat has no male equivalent. Comparing a woman's percentage against a man's
      scale is simply the wrong comparison.
    </p>

    <h2>What this cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>
      It is an estimate from a tape, typically within a few percentage points of a DEXA scan and
      least reliable at the extremes. It says nothing about where fat is stored internally, which
      is what actually drives metabolic risk. Its real value is <em>consistency</em>: measured
      the same way each month, the direction it moves is far more trustworthy than any single
      reading. This is general information, not medical advice — talk to a clinician before
      acting on it.
    </p>
    <p>
      For related figures see the <a href="/health/bmi-calculator">BMI calculator</a> and the
      <a href="/health/ideal-weight-calculator">ideal weight calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
